REPAIR research/non-ml-math-remaining-81-2026-10-18-owner-step2-car-exterior-completion-resolution/proposed/items/lem-choice-free-sequential-hilbert-completion-and-extension.md: passes only after auto-repair — adopt the canonical form:
  | **Given:** A complex inner-product space $X$, and, for the extension assertion, a complex Banach space $Y$ and a bounded linear $T:X\to Y$ with $\|Tx\|\le C\|x\|$.
  | 
  | [F1] The bounded operator space $\mathcal B(X,\mathbb C)$ has the unit-ball operator norm and is Banach because $\mathbb C$ is complete ([[def-space-of-bounded-linear-operators]], [[def-operator-norm]], [[thm-bounded-operator-space-is-banach]], [[thm-complex-plane-is-complete]]). The proof uses uniquely determined pointwise limits, not a family of arbitrary choices.
  | 
  | [F2] Cauchy–Schwarz gives $|\langle x,y\rangle|\le\|x\|\|y\|$; the original norm satisfies the parallelogram law. A complex norm satisfying that law determines a unique first-variable-linear inner product by polarization ([[thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space]], [[thm-parallelogram-law]], [[thm-jordan-von-neumann-polarization]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]).
  | 
  | [F3] Hartogs' theorem gives, for each set $A$, an ordinal $h(A)$ that does not inject into $A$. Transfinite recursion and induction hold in ZF without Choice ([[thm-hartogs]], [[thm-transfinite-recursion]], [[thm-transfinite-induction]]).
  | 
  | [F4] Replacement collects a family of uniquely specified values into a function ([[def-axiom-schema-of-replacement]]). Power Set, Separation and Union are available as in the ZF hypotheses of [F3].
  | 
  | [F5] A norm completion is a Banach space with a dense linear isometric embedding, and Hilbert completeness here means convergence of Cauchy sequences ([[def-completion-of-a-normed-space]], [[def-hilbert-space]]).
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | 1.1 Let $D=\mathcal B(X,\mathbb C)$ and give its conjugate vector space $\overline D$ the same addition and norm, with scalar action $\lambda\cdot_{\overline D}\varphi=\overline\lambda\varphi$. It is Banach by [F1]. Define $j(x)(y)=\langle y,x\rangle$. This is a bounded linear functional of $y$ by [F2]. Since $j(\lambda x)=\overline\lambda j(x)=\lambda\cdot_{\overline D}j(x)$, the map $j:X\to\overline D$ is complex-linear. Cauchy–Schwarz gives $\|j(x)\|\le\|x\|$; if $x\ne0$, evaluation at $y=x/\|x\|$ gives $|j(x)(y)|=\|x\|$, so equality holds; it also holds at zero. Thus $j$ is an isometry. [F1, F2, given, construct, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.1 Put $S=\overline{jX}$ in $\overline D$. It is a vector subspace: approximating two points within an arbitrary positive tolerance gives approximants for their sum, and approximating one point does the same for each scalar multiple. These are finite choices for fixed points and tolerance. It is complete because a Cauchy sequence has a limit in $\overline D$ by [F1], and that limit lies in the closed set $S$. The norm of $S$ satisfies the parallelogram law without selecting approximants for a whole sequence of tolerances. Indeed, for $u,v\in S$ and each $0<\delta\le1$, take $x,y\in X$ with $\|u-jx\|,\|v-jy\|<\delta$. The reverse triangle inequality and $|a^2-b^2|=|a-b|(a+b)$ show that the absolute value of the parallelogram defect at $(u,v)$ is at most $12\delta(\|u\|+\|v\|+1)$, since the defect at $(jx,jy)$ is zero by [F2]. This bound for every $\delta$ forces the defect to vanish. Polarization [F2] therefore equips $S$ with its unique compatible inner product. Since $j$ is linear and isometric, polarization shows this pairing restricts to the original pairing on $X$. Thus $S$ is an ambient Hilbert space and $jX$ is dense in $S$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, construct, algebra]
  | 
  | 3.1 For $A\subseteq S$, define $L(A)$ to be the set of limits in $S$ of all Cauchy sequences with terms in $A$. The set of sequences is a subset of $A^{\mathbb N}$, and each has a unique limit because $S$ is complete, so [F4] makes $L(A)$ a well-defined subset of $S$. Constant sequences give $A\subseteq L(A)$. Let $\kappa=h(\mathcal P(S))$, and use [F3] to define, through stage $\kappa$, $$A_0=jX,\qquad A_{\alpha+1}=L(A_\alpha),\qquad A_\lambda=\bigcup_{\alpha<\lambda}A_\alpha\quad(\lambda\text{ a nonzero limit ordinal}).$$ The chain is increasing. If $A_{\alpha+1}\ne A_\alpha$ for every $\alpha<\kappa$, then for $\alpha<\beta<\kappa$ one has $A_\alpha\subsetneq A_{\alpha+1}\subseteq A_\beta$, so $\alpha\mapsto A_\alpha$ injects $\kappa$ into $\mathcal P(S)$, contradicting the choice of $\kappa$. Let $\theta<\kappa$ be the least stage with $A_{\theta+1}=A_\theta$. This uses Hartogs of the power set, not a selection of one new point at each stage. [F3, F4, step 2.1, construct, induction, contradiction]
  | 
  | 4.1 Each $A_\alpha$ is a vector subspace. At a successor, for two points choose two witnessing sequences in $A_\alpha$; their sum and scalar multiples converge to the corresponding sum and scalar multiple in $S$, so these belong to $L(A_\alpha)$. Only two witnesses for a fixed pair are used, not a family indexed by all points. At a limit, any two points occur in two earlier stages, whose larger ordinal stage contains both; the union is therefore a vector subspace. Transfinite induction proves the assertion. Set $X^{\mathrm{seq}}=A_\theta$ with the restricted pairing. Every Cauchy sequence in it converges in $S$, and the fixed-point equality puts that limit back in $A_\theta$. Hence it is Hilbert. Since $jX$ is dense in $S$ and $jX\subseteq A_\theta$, it is dense in $A_\theta$ in the subspace norm. Thus $(X^{\mathrm{seq}},j)$ is a norm completion. Any sequentially closed subset of $S$ containing $jX$ contains every $A_\alpha$ by induction, so this hull is the least such subset. [F3, F5, step 2.1, step 3.1, induction, algebra]
  | 
  | 4.2 Start with $T_0(jx)=Tx$ on $A_0$. Suppose $T_\alpha:A_\alpha\to Y$ is linear with bound $C$. For $u\in A_{\alpha+1}$, there is a Cauchy sequence $(u_n)$ in $A_\alpha$ converging to $u$ in $S$. The sequence $T_\alpha u_n$ is Cauchy by its bound and has a limit in $Y$. If $(v_n)$ is another witness for $u$, then $\|T_\alpha u_n-T_\alpha v_n\|\le C\|u_n-v_n\|\to0$, so the two limits agree. Therefore the relation “$y$ is the limit of the image of a witnessing sequence for $u$” has exactly one value $y$ for each $u$. Replacement [F4] defines a function $T_{\alpha+1}$ with these values, without choosing one witnessing sequence for every $u$. Constant sequences show $T_{\alpha+1}|_{A_\alpha}=T_\alpha$. Taking limits in $\|T_\alpha u_n\|\le C\|u_n\|$ gives the same bound on $A_{\alpha+1}$. [F4, given, step 3.1, construct, algebra]
  | 
  | 5.1 For $u,v\in A_{\alpha+1}$, choose two witnesses $(u_n),(v_n)$ in $A_\alpha$. The witnesses $(u_n+v_n)$ and $(\lambda u_n)$ and uniqueness of limits give $T_{\alpha+1}(u+v)=T_{\alpha+1}u+T_{\alpha+1}v$ and $T_{\alpha+1}(\lambda u)=\lambda T_{\alpha+1}u$. At a limit ordinal take the union of the compatible preceding functions. This union is a function, and its linearity and bound follow in one common earlier stage for every fixed pair of arguments. Transfinite recursion [F3] therefore produces the compatible bounded linear maps through stage $\theta$, giving $\widetilde T=T_\theta$. [F3, F4, step 4.1, step 4.2, construct, induction, algebra]
  | 
  | 6.1 If $U,V:X^{\mathrm{seq}}\to Y$ are bounded linear extensions of $T$ with respective bounds $C_U,C_V$, then for each $u$ and each $\delta>0$, density supplies one $x\in X$ with $\|u-jx\|<\delta$. Since $Ujx=Vjx$, one has $\|Uu-Vu\|\le(C_U+C_V)\delta$. Hence $Uu=Vu$ for every $u$. No simultaneous family of approximants is used. This proves uniqueness, including uniqueness among extensions with a different bound. [step 4.1, step 5.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 7.1 If $X$ is already Cauchy complete, $jX$ is sequentially closed in $S$: any Cauchy sequence in $jX$ has the unique inverse sequence in $X$ under $j$, and the latter converges in $X$; its image is the unique ambient limit. Thus $L(jX)=jX$, $\theta=0$, and $X^{\mathrm{seq}}=jX$. In particular completing a Hilbert space by this construction changes nothing. Together with steps 4.1–4.1 this proves all assertions without a choice axiom. [F4, F5, step 1.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 5.1, step 6.1, algebra, discharge-construct] ∎

